rule base pausing where

-- HANDING THE PROOF OVER AT A PLACEHOLDER — four lines, in user space.
--
--   :consult examples/demo/Demo08Pausing.thena.rules
--
-- The shipped base has two clauses of `elaborate` for a hole you wrote
-- yourself, and both have an EMPTY body: "those elaborate just by not
-- elaborating. The hole is already there." The hole is then still open when
-- the declaration finishes, so the declaration is refused and the module is
-- rolled back — there is nothing left to work on.
--
-- This base adds one clause that hands control to you instead. `:consult`
-- puts it on top of the search stack, so it is reached before the shipped
-- one, and then:
--
--   declare two : Nat ; two = succ _
--   a placeholder: fill it and `yield` to carry on
--   (yield to hand control back)
--
-- …and you are standing on the placeholder's own hole, with the declaration's
-- own context in scope. Fill it, type `yield`, and the elaboration carries on
-- and the declaration lands.
--
-- **IT DOES NOT SURVIVE A MODULE LOAD, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE OF ITEM
-- `~mgqwb`.** The rule fires when a `.thena` file reaches a placeholder — the
-- message prints — but a module elaborates in an isolated session, and the
-- suspended machine is gone by the time the prompt comes back: `yield`
-- answers `nothing has yielded`. So this works at the prompt and only at the
-- prompt.
--
-- There is no `Nat` in the prelude; declare one, or consult this alongside a
-- module that has the datatype you want to use.

rule elaborate t :- when focus-is-hole (surface-is-placeholder t)
  do yield "a placeholder: fill it and `yield` to carry on"
